## What to check first

Record a Chrome DevTools Performance trace with **4× CPU throttling** while scrolling. Jank on mid-range Android is almost always one of: non-composited properties animating, too many `ScrollTrigger` instances, permanently promoted layers, oversized images, or a canvas/WebGL loop running during scroll. Look for Layout/Paint and >50 ms long tasks inside a scroll frame.

## Fixes, in likely order of impact

1. **Non-composited properties.** Animate `transform` and `opacity` only. Red flags in a scroll tween: `width`, `height`, `top`/`left`, `filter`, `backdrop-filter`, `box-shadow`. Replace progress-bar `width` with `scaleX`, a `top` shift with `translateY`, and drop `filter`/blur entirely on mobile — each one forces layout or paint every frame.
2. **ScrollTrigger count and cleanup.** Check `ScrollTrigger.getAll().length` in the console. Dozens of one-per-card triggers doing `onUpdate` work is a classic cause. Use `ScrollTrigger.batch()` with `once: true`, and kill everything on route change with `useGSAP` scope **plus** `mm.revert()` — scope alone does not kill a matchMedia context. No pinning, scrubbing or ScrollSmoother on touch; keep pin/scrub behind `(min-width: 768px)`.
3. **`will-change` used wrongly.** Applying `will-change` permanently to every card exhausts Android GPU layer memory and makes things worse. Set it narrowly just before the tween and remove it on complete.
4. **Image sizes.** Serve correctly sized WebP/AVIF (not a 4000 px hero scaled down), give images explicit `width`/`height`, and lazy-load below the fold. Trigger positions going stale as images decode causes repeated refreshes — call `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` once after load, never on scroll.
5. **WebGL/canvas layers.** One per page maximum, lazy-mounted below the fold, `aria-hidden="true"` and `pointer-events: none`. Pause its rAF loop when off-screen; a canvas redrawing under a scroll animation will drop frames on a low-tier GPU.

